# Symphony's own settings. Copy to symphony.env — it is gitignored.
#
# OPTIONAL: every value below has a working default, so the file-queue tracker
# runs with no symphony.env at all. You need one for the GitLab tracker, or to
# change the log level.
#
# Read by ./symphony only, and deliberately NOT part of the root .env:
# docker-compose.yml hands that file to the agent's container wholesale
# (`env_file: - .env`), so anything in it is visible to the agent. This file
# is passed to compose via --env-file only, which drives ${VAR}
# interpolation and puts nothing inside a container directly — that is the
# mechanism that keeps the orchestrator's Reporter token out of the agent's
# environment, not a filing convention. No SYMPHONY_* key may appear in .env
# or in projects/<name>/.env; `symphony check` warns if one does.
#
# Same parser rule as .env: comments on their OWN lines, never inline after a
# value.
#
# Read the README's "The security model" first. The containment that
# matters is the SCOPE OF THE GITLAB TOKEN (its role and its per-project
# reach), not these settings.

SYMPHONY_LOG_LEVEL=info

# === GitLab tracker ===========================================================
# Set this ONLY when a project's config/WORKFLOW.md says `tracker: { kind:
# gitlab }`. Leave it blank for the file queue and the symphony container
# keeps its stronger posture: no credentials and no egress whatsoever.
#
# It must be a GROUP access token with the REPORTER role and `api` scope,
# covering a sandbox group that holds several projects — the fallback for
# when several symphony projects would otherwise each need their own token.
# The normal case is narrower than this: a PROJECT access token, same role
# and scope, set per-project in projects/<name>/symphony.env, which is read
# after this file and wins. `symphony check` tells you which of the two a
# given project is actually running with, so it is never silent either way.
#
# `api` is full API access for that project (there is no issues-only scope);
# the ROLE is what constrains it. Reporter can read and write issues and
# CANNOT push code — the agent's Developer token (GITLAB_PAT, in the
# project's own .env) does that, in the other container. Two tokens, neither
# able to do the other's job — see the README's "Two tokens, neither able to
# do the other's job".
SYMPHONY_GITLAB_TOKEN=

# Egress is not a setting here: ./symphony derives the route from
# tracker.kind in the project's WORKFLOW.md and puts the orchestrator behind
# squid only when the tracker is gitlab, leaving it with no route off-host
# otherwise. Override with SYMPHONY_HTTP_PROXY only for a stack where squid is
# not the way out (e.g. a host-level proxy already in front of everything).
# SYMPHONY_HTTP_PROXY=

# There is deliberately no SYMPHONY_QUEUE_PATH / SYMPHONY_WORKSPACES_PATH /
# SYMPHONY_CONFIG_PATH here. Those are derived per-project from
# projects/<name>/{queue,workspaces,config} by ./symphony itself — a shared
# concrete default for any of them is exactly the mistake that would collapse
# every project's queue, workspaces or config onto the same directory.
